1	#!/usr/bin/env node
2	// The claudinite-fleet-sheepdog pack's fleet ROSTER sweep — one walk of the fleet answering the
3	// enforcer's two standing questions about every repo under the owner:
4	//
5	//   is this repo a MEMBER?           → `fleet-adoption` issues   (adoption-issues.mjs)
6	//   is that membership still MEANING anything?
7	//                                    → `fleet-drift` issues      (drift-issues.mjs)
8	//
9	// Run by this pack's `fleet-roster` scheduled task, whose worker calls `main()` below
10	// as the task's `code_work` — Action-side inside the enforcer repo's scheduler workflow,
11	// where the FLEET_GITHUB_TOKEN the task declares in `required_secrets` is reachable as
12	// ordinary environment. Still runnable by hand (`node check-fleet-roster.mjs`) via the
13	// CLI guard at the foot.
14	//
15	// WHY ONE WALK (#788). The two questions were two tasks, and each carried its own
16	// enumeration, its own owner filter, its own empty-enumeration guard, its own
17	// home/canon/archived/fork/excluded skips and its own declaration read per repo. The
18	// freshness half's header claimed it "takes coverage as given" while in fact
19	// re-deriving it, on another cadence, in another process — so the two classifications
20	// could disagree and did: `exclude` was applied at different points (an excluded repo
21	// that still carried a declaration read `covered` to one and `out of scope` to the
22	// other), and each half's `unknown` failed its own run knowing nothing of the other's.
23	// The roster is now built ONCE and both halves consume it, so a repo has one
24	// membership verdict and the report has one failure boundary.
25	//
26	// WHAT IS STILL SEPARATE, deliberately: the two ISSUE FAMILIES. Coverage and drift
27	// close on unrelated conditions and carry unrelated remedies, so each keeps its own
28	// label, its own convergence policy and its own section of the report, in its own
29	// module. This file owns the walk and nothing else.
30	//
31	// UNKNOWN IS PER-QUESTION, which is the one behaviour the merge changes. A declaration
32	// that cannot be read or parsed is unknown to BOTH halves — it is the input they share.
33	// A mount probe that fails (the scheduler read, canon's compare) is unknown to the
34	// FRESHNESS half alone: the coverage half already read that repo's declaration
35	// successfully and keeps its verdict. Either kind of unknown fails the run, so nothing
36	// is downgraded to silence; what changes is that a compare outage no longer erases a
37	// repo from the coverage picture.
38	//
39	// Two rules kept from both predecessors:
40	//   - an indeterminate repo is UNKNOWN, never a finding — no issue is opened for it,
41	//     and the run fails so the cause escalates;
42	//   - an unreadable/absent claudinite-fleet-sheepdog config aborts the sweep — absence is not consent
43	//     to cover everything with no exclusions.
44	//
45	// Dependency-free (global fetch, Node 20+); read-only toward every repo except the
46	// enforcer, where it writes the two issue families and their labels.
47	
48	import { appendFileSync } from 'node:fs';
49	import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
50	import { makeGh, paged, readDeclaration, isDormant, ensureLabel, DECLARATION } from '../../fleet-api.mjs';
51	import { parseSheepdogConfig } from '../../fleet-config.mjs';
52	import { missingFleetTokenError } from '../../fleet-token.mjs';
53	import * as adoption from './adoption-issues.mjs';
54	import * as drift from './drift-issues.mjs';
55	
56	// --- the roster walk ----------------------------------------------------------
57	
58	// One entry per repo under the owner, carrying FACTS rather than a verdict: the two
59	// halves disagree about what some of those facts mean (an excluded repo is still
60	// `covered` to the coverage question and out of scope to the freshness one), and a
61	// single pre-chewed state would have to pick a winner. The views below do the
62	// interpreting, each for its own question.
63	//
64	// Reads per repo: the declaration, once — the read both halves needed and each used to
65	// make separately. Plus two more (scheduler workflow, canon compare) for the members
66	// the freshness half actually measures, and canon's version numbers, which the shared
67	// `canonVersions` reader fetches once per distinct pack across the whole walk.
